is trend as the ZC sequence proves to be a reliable sequence design. Proposal 6: RAN1 to study PRACH enhancement using 5G sequence (ZC) as the starting point. A common pool of preambles is supported for each cell, e.g., for LTE and NR, up to 64 preambles are available for UE selection (contention-based) or assigned by the base station (gNB) (contention-free) depending on the zero-correlation zone parameter or cell radius. However, given the expected increase in number of devices in 6G and RACH partitioning possibility as part of early identification of feature, the current preamble allocation or pool per cell may be constrained as increased number of devices from different applications are expected to perform initial access, e.g., 6G-MBB, IoT or NTN devices leading to a higher number of collisions. To increase the preamble pool, while continuing to maintain the based ZC preamble design, increasing the number of preambles needs to be studied. The preamble pool can be expanded through sequence‑spreading or cover‑code approaches, such as: 1) m-sequence spread ZC 2) OCC spread ZC (masked ZC sequence) 3) Third-order polynomial (P3) spread ZC Table 1 shows the basic simulation assumptions used to compare various PRACH designs based on sequence spreading. Table 1: Exemplary Simulation Assumptions Figure 2: Missed Detection Performance for different PRACH designs with different BW allocations and no repetitions, UE mobility = 3km/h (cell radius = 1.4km) It can be noted that the Normal ZC, m-sequence spread ZC and third-order polynomial (P3) spread ZC display similar coverage performance for a single UE for the target missed detection probability of 1%, however frequency-based OCC provides a notable gain over the three other schemes in the order of approximately 2 dB. The improved

{"chunk_id":"R1-2602878-007","collection":"ran1_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Ericsson"],"tdocNumber":"R1-2602878","title":"Channel coding for 6GR"},"point_id":"17167523837877523875"}
During RAN1#123 and RAN1#124, several evaluation assumptions were agreed for study of LDPC code extension. While computation complexity in terms of operations count is agreed to compare LDPC code performance, such operations count is not the only factor in determination of overall complexity. The performance-complexity trade-off aspects need to reflect practical implementation aspects apart from just the computational operations count. During previous meetings, RAN1 discussed some practical aspects regarding 6GR networks handling both 6GR as well as 5G traffic. It should be clear that the implementations at both UE and NW side still must consider dual-RAT (5G and 6G) support and this needs to be considered in the overall discussion on channel coding. Note that in this context, dual-RAT support on the UE side does not imply simultaneous 5G and 6G operation – it simply refers to the UE implementation which is able to support 6G operation as well as 5G operation. On the network side, dual RAT operations mean the gNB may serve some UEs operating in 5G and other UEs operating in 6G. In terms of overall baseband processing capacity on the network side, the impact of an additional 6GR-only decoder coexisting with current 5G decoders translates directly in either increased implementation complexity/chip area or decreased gNB processing capacity for both 6GR and 5G, since some of the chip area is now dedicated to 6GR baseband processing, and thus only a specific mix of 6GR and 5G traffic could be potentially handled. On the other hand, by maximizing commonalities between the existing 5G codes and the new BG3, the impact on processing capacity can be minimized. Complexity analysis of 6G LDPC decoder takes into account the implementation complexity reflecting the support of BG3 decoder, which is on top of the existing BG1 and BG2. This can lead to added chip area or reduction in processing capacity of other functions (e.g. other baseband functions). Regarding area efficiency for LDPC, from last meeting’s discussion, it is apparent that a unified area efficiency metric may be difficult to converge on. It is also difficult to align implementation-level details as the assumption may be different for different companies, including how it is accommodated on top of existing base graphs. For instance, taking double-edge as one issue, while proponents think that it is easy to accommodate in one type of decoder implementations, there are others that do not agree with such assessment and have concerns with it. Likewise, the maximum lifting size (between 192 and 384) has similar debate. Overall, we think that the discussion can be spent on studying BG3 parameters, narrowing down choices based on some high-level principles (like the discussion in section 2.3.1.2). Regarding energy efficiency for LDPC, the overall UE power consumption depends on many functions including RF that can consume more energy (vs baseband), and even in baseband there are several other functions than just LDPC. Hence the overall energy impacts may be limited in typical system operation.

{"chunk_id":"R1-2504881-38_213-header","collection":"ran1_cr_chunks","metadata":{"source":"Moderator (Samsung)","specNumber":"38.213","tdocNumber":"R1-2504881","title":"CR for HARQ-ACK aspects of Rel-17 multi-PDSCH scheduling"},"point_id":"17319744792810461287"}
ook” (FG 18-9), in terms of determining the number of times that a serving cell needs to be counted (and be run through the pseudo-code) when a UE receives more than one unicast DL DCIs for a serving cell in a same PDCCH monitoring occasion (MO). However, as can be inferred from the CRs and the discussion summary document in R1-2310512, the clarification only considered the case of single-PDSCH scheduling, while the case of a DCI 1_1 with multi-PDSCH scheduling as per Rel-17 was not considered. In fact, if the current specifications (as per CR in R1-2310666) is directly applied to Rel-17 multi-PDSCH scheduling, the UE needs to count (and run the pseudo-code for) a corresponding serving cell based on the number of scheduled PDSCHs. Such counting would be against the DAI principle agreed in Rel-17 multi-PDSCH scheduling, that DAI counting is per DCI format, and not per scheduled PDSCH. The issue is further described in a discussion paper R1-2503547. Therefore, clarification is necessary that the previous agreed CRs were for the case of single-PDSCH scheduling, and that counting for the case of multi-PDSCH scheduling is based on the number of DCI formats received for a serving cell in a same PDCCH MO. Summary of change: Clarify that, for a Type-2 HARQ-ACK codebook: when a UE receives multiple DCI formats in a same PDCCH MO for scheduling PDSCHs on a same serving cell, and at least one of the multiple DCI formats schedules multiple PDSCHs on the serving cell, the UE counts (and runs the Type-2 HARQ-ACK pseudo-code for) the serving cells a same number of times as the number of DCI formats – and not the number of scheduled PDSCH receptions. Consequences if not approved: Incorrect specifications for Type-2 HARQ-ACK codebook with multi-PDSCH scheduling.

